import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Stack"
  description="One headless laptop, lid closed, running the whole homelab on Proxmox and a pile of unprivileged containers. Self-hosted and self-reliant."
  windowTitle="Stack"
  active="stack"
>
  <div class="room-head">
    <h1 class="room-title">The Stack</h1>
    <p class="room-lead">One laptop, lid closed, doing the work of a rack.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The whole homelab runs on a single System76 laptop named minas-tirith, lid shut, tucked on
      a shelf. Its battery is the UPS. It's a 24-core i9 with 62 gigs of RAM that spends most of
      its life bored, load hovering around a quarter of one core, because the job was never
      horsepower. The job is staying up, and staying mine.
    </p>
    <p>
      On top of Proxmox it's a stack of unprivileged LXC containers, one service to a container,
      so a mess in any one of them stays in that one. Everything on it is self-hosted and mine,
      with no telemetry I didn't turn on myself. When it goes sideways at 2am the person who fixes
      it is me, which is the entire point.
    </p>

    <h3>What it runs</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Networking and access.</strong> Technitium handles DNS and DHCP for the house.
      Caddy sits out front with a wildcard certificate, Authelia guards the things worth guarding
      with single sign-on and a TOTP prompt, and a Cloudflare tunnel exposes only the handful of
      services that are meant to face outward.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Watching it.</strong> Grafana, Loki, and Prometheus for the metrics and logs,
      Beszel and Uptime Kuma for the at-a-glance "is it alive," and NetAlertX keeping an inventory
      of everything on the network so a new MAC address doesn't go unnoticed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Keeping it honest.</strong> A Wazuh SIEM chews through the logs, CrowdSec turns
      away the obvious junk at the edge, and a honeypot or two that page me the moment anything on the
      LAN touches something it has no business touching.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>The useful stuff.</strong> A Matrix server for chat that's actually mine, Forgejo
      for git, and ntfy as the single sink every other service shouts into when it needs my
      attention.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Not losing it.</strong> Proxmox Backup Server takes deduplicated snapshots onto a
      ZFS pool, and an offsite copy goes out from there. Backups you have never restored are a
      rumor, so that path gets tested, not assumed.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Not here on purpose.</strong> This website. Anything the public can reach runs on
      droplets, kept off the home network by design, so the open internet never has a route back
      to the machine my actual life runs on.
    </p>

    <h3>The desktop</h3>
    <p>
      The machine I actually sit at runs CachyOS, a performance-tuned Arch, under Hyprland with
      the Noctalia shell stretched across an ultrawide. If the front door of this site feels
      familiar, that's why: it's dressed as the desktop I'm typing this on.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Where the work happens.</strong> Mostly the terminal, I'm a terminal guy through
      and through: Ghostty with zsh underneath and nano for quick edits. It's all Rust now, so a
      JetBrains IDE when I want the full weight of one, and VSCodium with the Microsoft telemetry
      filed off the rest of the time.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Where AI earns its keep.</strong> Claude Code does the heavy lifting I point it at,
      kept on rails by Cadence for planning and Hindsight for memory that survives between
      sessions. Useful exactly where it's useful, which is the whole argument I make about the
      stuff.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Staying in touch.</strong> The same Matrix server that runs the house is where my
      family talks, so the group chat isn't a product someone else gets to mine.
    </p>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
